      "id": "word-tipo--guy",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tipo",
      "answerEn": "guy; man",
      "noteEn": "In informal speech, tipo can refer to a man as “guy.” In other contexts, it can instead mean a type or kind.",
      "example": { "es": "Ese tipo vive cerca.", "en": "That guy lives nearby." },
      "tags": ["word", "person", "informal", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-pegado--very-close",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "pegado",
      "answerEn": "right up against; very close",
      "noteEn": "Pegado comes from pegar, “to stick.” With estar and a, it can describe someone or something pressed against or extremely close to another person or thing.",
      "example": { "es": "El armario está pegado a la pared.", "en": "The wardrobe is right up against the wall." },
      "tags": ["word", "adjective", "proximity", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-echaré--take-a-look",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "echaré",
      "answerEn": "I'll take, as part of echar un vistazo",
      "noteEn": "Echar has many meanings, including “to throw” or “to put.” In echar un vistazo, it forms the conventional expression “to take a look.” The ending -é marks a future action by “I.”",
      "example": { "es": "Echaré un vistazo al informe.", "en": "I'll take a look at the report." },
      "tags": ["word", "echar", "future", "fixed expression", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-vistazo", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "vistazo", "answerEn": "glance; quick look", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "noun", "A2"] },
    {
      "id": "word-su--his",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "su",
      "answerEn": "his",
      "noteEn": "Su agrees with the possessed thing, not with the owner. The same Spanish form can mean his, her, its, their, or formal your; the surrounding context identifies the owner.",
      "example": { "es": "Carlos abre su portátil.", "en": "Carlos opens his laptop." },
      "tags": ["word", "possessive", "third person", "A1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-encendido--powered-on", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "encendido", "answerEn": "on; powered on", "noteEn": "For a device, encendido means switched on or running. It is the describing form made from encender, “to turn on.”", "example": { "es": "El ordenador está encendido.", "en": "The computer is on." }, "tags": ["word", "technology", "state", "A2"] },
    {
      "id": "word-probablemente",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "probablemente",
      "answerEn": "probably",
      "noteEn": "Probablemente combines probable with -mente to describe a claim as likely rather than certain.",
      "example": { "es": "Probablemente llegará tarde.", "en": "She will probably arrive late." },
      "tags": ["word", "adverb", "probability", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-asiento", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "asiento", "answerEn": "seat", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "vehicle", "noun", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-de--rear-position",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "de",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; links an item to atrás to describe it as the back or rear one",
      "noteEn": "Spanish can put de atrás after an object to identify the one positioned at the back. Natural English usually puts “back” before the noun.",
      "example": { "es": "Usamos la puerta de atrás.", "en": "We use the back door." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "position", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-se--autoconectarse",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "se",
      "answerEn": "itself; marks autoconectarse, to connect automatically",
      "noteEn": "Autoconectarse uses a matching small word for the thing that connects: me, te, se, nos, or os. Use se with one third-person thing that connects itself or connects automatically.",
      "example": { "es": "El teléfono se autoconecta a la red.", "en": "The phone connects to the network automatically." },
      "tags": ["word", "pronoun", "pronominal verb", "technology", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-autoconecta--automatically-connects",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "autoconecta",
      "answerEn": "connects automatically; auto-connects",
      "noteEn": "Autoconecta is a present form used with one device or connection. With se, it describes that thing connecting without the user starting the connection manually.",
      "example": { "es": "La tableta se autoconecta.", "en": "The tablet connects automatically." },
      "tags": ["word", "technology", "present", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-buscamos--look-for", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "buscamos", "answerEn": "we look for; we search for", "noteEn": "Buscamos is the present form of buscar for a group that includes the speaker.", "example": { "es": "Buscamos una solución.", "en": "We are looking for a solution." }, "tags": ["word", "buscar", "present", "first person plural", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-exploit--security",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "exploit",
      "answerEn": "security exploit; a way to take advantage of a software flaw",
      "noteEn": "Spanish computing language borrows the English noun exploit. It names code or a method that uses a vulnerability to make a system do something unintended.",
      "example": { "es": "El equipo corrigió el exploit.", "en": "The team fixed the exploit." },
      "tags": ["word", "computing", "security", "loanword", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-remoto--network",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "remoto",
      "answerEn": "remote; usable over a network without physical access",
      "noteEn": "In computing, remoto describes access or an action performed from another machine or location through a network.",
      "example": { "es": "El servidor permite acceso remoto.", "en": "The server allows remote access." },
      "tags": ["word", "computing", "adjective", "B1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-instalamos", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "instalamos", "answerEn": "we install", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "instalar", "present", "first person plural", "A1"] },
    { "id": "word-sintetizador", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "sintetizador", "answerEn": "synthesizer", "noteEn": "A sintetizador de voz is a speech synthesizer: software or hardware that produces spoken audio.", "example": { "es": "El sintetizador produce una voz artificial.", "en": "The synthesizer produces an artificial voice." }, "tags": ["word", "technology", "audio", "B1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-probamos--try-option",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "probamos",
      "answerEn": "we try; we test an option",
      "noteEn": "Probamos is a present form of probar for a group including the speaker. With con, it introduces a person, thing, or approach chosen to see whether it works.",
      "example": { "es": "Probamos con otra clave.", "en": "We try another key." },
      "tags": ["word", "probar", "present", "first person plural", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-psicológico", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "psicológico", "answerEn": "psychological", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-oscuro--sinister", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "oscuro", "answerEn": "dark; sinister", "noteEn": "Oscuro can describe low light literally. Applied to an idea or action, it can instead suggest something disturbing, sinister, or morally grim.", "example": { "es": "La historia tiene un final oscuro.", "en": "The story has a dark ending." }, "tags": ["word", "adjective", "figurative", "B1"] },
    { "id": "word-ella--subject", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "ella", "answerEn": "she", "noteEn": "Ella is the feminine singular subject pronoun. Spanish often leaves subject pronouns unstated, but ella can be included for clarity or emphasis.", "example": { "es": "Ella vive aquí.", "en": "She lives here." }, "tags": ["word", "subject pronoun", "feminine", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-fuera--counterfactual-ser",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "fuera",
      "answerEn": "were; were the case, in an unreal condition",
      "noteEn": "Fuera is a special form of ser used for a hypothetical situation. In si no fuera por..., it introduces something without which the result would be different. Do not confuse it with fuera meaning “outside.”",
      "example": { "es": "Si fuera fácil, todos lo harían.", "en": "If it were easy, everyone would do it." },
      "tags": ["word", "ser", "hypothetical", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-por--fixed-si-no-fuera-por",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "por",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; completes si no fuera por, if it weren't for",
      "noteEn": "In the fixed counterfactual expression si no fuera por, por introduces the person or thing whose presence changes the result.",
      "example": { "es": "Si no fuera por Ana, llegaríamos tarde.", "en": "If it weren't for Ana, we would arrive late." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "counterfactual", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-seguiría--would-still-be",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "seguiría",
      "answerEn": "would still be; would remain",
      "noteEn": "Seguir normally means “to continue.” Before a word describing a condition, it means to remain or still be in that condition. The ending -ía makes this an imagined “would” result.",
      "example": { "es": "Sin ayuda, la puerta seguiría cerrada.", "en": "Without help, the door would still be closed." },
      "tags": ["word", "seguir", "conditional", "state", "B1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-viva--alive", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "viva", "answerEn": "alive (feminine singular)", "noteEn": "Viva is the feminine singular form of vivo and matches one feminine person or thing.", "example": { "es": "La planta sigue viva.", "en": "The plant is still alive." }, "tags": ["word", "adjective", "state", "A2"] },
    {
      "id": "phrase-estar-pegado-a",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "estar pegado/pegada a alguien o algo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone or something is stuck to, pressed against, or extremely close to another thing?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of estar, then pegado, pegada, pegados, or pegadas, followed by a and the reference person or thing.",
      "noteEn": "Estar changes for the person and time. Pegado changes to match the person or thing being described. A introduces what it is attached or very close to. The expression can describe literal contact or, from context, unusually close proximity.",
      "example": { "es": "La mesa está pegada a la pared.", "en": "The table is right up against the wall." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "proximity", "estar", "adjective", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-echar-un-vistazo",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "echar un vistazo a algo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone takes a quick look at something?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of echar followed by un vistazo; use a before the thing being viewed when it is stated.",
      "noteEn": "Echar has many literal meanings, but echar un vistazo is a conventional expression for looking briefly. Only echar changes for the person and time: echo, echas, echó, echaré. Un vistazo stays together.",
      "example": { "es": "Voy a echar un vistazo al mapa.", "en": "I'm going to take a look at the map." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "echar un vistazo", "looking", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-adverbs-mente",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "adjetivo femenino singular + -mente",
      "questionEn": "How do you turn many describing words into words such as “quickly,” “clearly,” or “probably”?",
      "answerEn": "Use the feminine singular form of the describing word and add -mente. If its masculine and feminine forms are identical, simply add -mente.",
      "noteEn": "For an adjective that changes, use its -a form: claro becomes clara and then claramente. For one form shared by both genders, probable becomes probablemente. The new word usually describes how an action happens or how the speaker judges a claim.",
      "example": { "es": "Lucía responde claramente.", "en": "Lucía answers clearly." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "adverbs", "mente", "word formation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-si-no-fuera-por",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "si no fuera por alguien o algo",
      "questionEn": "How do you say “if it weren't for” a person or thing that changes the outcome?",
      "answerEn": "Use si no fuera por, then name the person or thing. Si no fuese por is an equally valid alternative.",
      "noteEn": "This expression presents an unreal alternative: without the named factor, the result would be different. Fuera and fuese are two forms of the same hypothetical verb here. The result commonly uses a Spanish “would” form ending in -ría.",
      "example": { "es": "Si no fuera por Ana, llegaríamos tarde.", "en": "If it weren't for Ana, we would arrive late." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "counterfactual", "si", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-remote-security-exploit",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "exploit remoto",
      "answerEn": "a method that uses a software vulnerability through a network, without physical access to the target device",
      "noteEn": "A remote exploit may let an attacker make a vulnerable machine run unintended actions or code from elsewhere. The comic relies on that idea when the nearby laptop is altered through its wireless connection.",
      "tags": ["concept", "computing", "cybersecurity", "remote exploit"]
    }
  ],
